 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Identifies the criteria the commissioner of environmental conservation should consider in publishing a list of high local environmental impact zones; provides that high local environmental impact zones are areas most adversely affected by existing environmental hazards.",
 "title": "Identifies the criteria the commissioner of environmental conservation should consider in publishing a list of high local environmental impact zones",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A1779",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A1779-2019",
     "printNo": "A1779",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 136\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 1779, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n      high local environmental impact communities\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This legislation  would  require  the  Commissioner  of  Environmental\nConservation  (Commissioner)  to biennially publish a list of high local\nenvironmental impact zones.  \"High  local  environmental  impact  zones\"\nwould be those census tracts, census blocks or nine-digit zip code areas\nmost  adversely  affected  by existing environmental hazards. The legis-\nlation would require that at a minimum, five percent of the state's five\ndigit zip code areas, in whole or in part, be identified as  high  local\nenvironmental  impact  zones.  In  defining the high local environmental\nimpact zones, the Commissioner would be directed to consider  at  least:\n(a)  the  quantities of toxic chemicals released in the area as reported\nin the Toxics Release Inventory (TRI); (b) emissions and  discharges  of\npollutants and storage of wastes; (c) sales and usage of pesticides; (d)\nimpairments  to  water  bodies; (e) presence of inactive hazardous waste\ndisposal sites; (f) discharges of  petroleum;  (g)  proximity  to  other\nzones  listed;  and  (h)  non-attainment  of  air quality standards. The\nCommissioner would be required to publish the list of high  local  envi-\nronmental  impact  zones  in  a  report which shall identify the factors\nconsidered and how the factors were weighted in making the designations.\nThe report would be required to be made available in print form  and  on\nthe  Department's  website  and  first  be published within two years of\nenactment.\n \n  This bill was vetoed in 2010 and many of the  issues  identified  with\nthe  previous  bill, including the redundancy to other DEC programs, the\nlack of access to information required by the bill,  and  the  potential\nfiscal  impact  on  DEC, have not been addressed. I share in the concern\nthat communities do not have access to data  on  facilities  located  in\ntheir  area  or  information  about  historical  discharges of potential\npollution. In that vein, DEC recently launched an interactive  map  that\nprovides  access to DEC documents and public data. Users are able to see\nand download permits, water quality reports and links to other DEC docu-\nments of interest to people wherever they may work, live or  play.  This\nmapping tool will also provide information about potential environmental\njustice  areas. Therefore, this main aspect of the bill has already been\naddressed.\n\n  However,  many  problems  present  with  the  2010  bill  remain.  For\ninstance, the bill would require DEC to locate data sets not held by the\nstate, regardless of whether such data sets are obtainable by DEC or can\nbe  incorporated into DEC's database. There are many requirements in the\nbill for data to be obtained by DEC that are not known.\n \n  I am equally concerned with the bill's  mandate  that  DEC  develop  a\nrating  protocol  to  determine which communities are more affected than\nothers.  This would be a staff intensive  endeavor  that  would  require\n \ntremendous  resources. During the permitting process for certain facili-\nties, modeling may be done to identify the area of maximum impact  of  a\nfacility and such area may not be in the same census tract, block or zip\ncode  as the location of the facility. Also, permits often have a desig-\nnated maximum potential to emit level that does not necessarily  reflect\nthe actual emissions from that facility.\n\n  The  potential to draw inappropriate conclusions about certain facili-\nties being located in a specific area or about the impacts such a facil-\nity or facilities may have on the health of a community cannot be  over-\nlooked.    Using  a  database  to  draw  conclusions  about the relative\nenvironmental health of an area presents  a  risk  of  misinterpretation\nbecause data on a particular area may change over time. Some discharges,\nwhen  in compliance with state regulatory requirements, may pose no risk\nof adverse environmental impacts. The resulting data, flawed as  it  may\nbe,  will  potentially  impact  property  values or create permitting or\nsiting problems for existing projects for no reason other than misappre-\nhension.\n \n  In light of the fact that one of the underlying goals of the bill  has\nalready  been materially met with DEC's recently launched \"DECinfo Loca-\ntor,\" and the fact that the remaining concerns with this bill  have  not\nbeen addressed, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
